## Context

These tools are the agent's only way into the observer, and the agent is the party least able to keep a rule: it forgets, retries, and reads a warning as a suggestion. So every rule the session start depends on is enforced in a tool reply the agent cannot misread, and the decisions that need the user (ending another session) go to the user through the agent, with the tool refusing what no approval can make safe.

Each tool runs inside the calling session's own server, which knows its observer session but sees other sessions only through what is stored on disk. A start comes at boot or as the one restart after a missed report; either way the user has to be able to see what is going on, including the session that blocks this one.

A conversation the user resumes after exiting or archiving keeps its host conversation id but arrives in a fresh server under a fresh observer session, knowing nothing of its own end. Only the records on disk show that this conversation ended earlier and whether another session has worked the project since; an older conversation that carries on regardless works from a stale picture of the project.

## Strategy

**Start finds or launches, then checks, then gives exactly one answer.** The observer comes up first, so even a halt or a hard stop carries a link the user can open. No port on the list is a blocker naming every port tried, and no check runs. Otherwise the check reads presence, ended marks and transcripts from disk, and the reply is the link (saying so when it reopened the session), a halt or a hard stop, never a mixed answer.

**The caller is its conversation, not its process.** The check joins the caller to its host conversation through its own presence and finds that conversation's earlier observer sessions. None of them ever halts the caller, and an ended mark on the latest of them makes the caller an ended session. Judging the fresh process alone would treat a resumed conversation as new and let a stale picture through. A hookless host has no join, so its caller is checked as new.

**An ended caller passes four tests, in order.** An end approved from another session is a hard stop; so is any session of the project, open or ended, started after the caller's conversation last started. Then another open session gets the usual halt. Otherwise the mark is cleared and the link given. Hard stops go first because no approval lifts them: halting first would spend an approval only to stop anyway. A hard stop writes nothing; the user continues in a new session.

**A halt names enough to decide on.** It gives the other session's state, last activity, quiet time with maybe stuck, and its pipeline and phase, or its host id when it never reported, and says plainly that nothing proceeds. The user cannot see that session otherwise, and a vague "another session may be active" invites the agent to carry on.

**Start marks what the transcripts show closed.** Another session the check finds closed from its transcript and still unmarked gets an ended mark, so a later resume of it meets the reopen rule instead of slipping back in as new. The write is best effort: a failure changes nothing in the reply, since the verdict holds on read anyway. Only start writes this mark, and never for the caller.

**Ending is a mark the user approves, never self-applied.** The tool marks another session of the caller's project ended and deletes nothing; ending one already ended changes nothing. One already judged closed from its transcript gets the transcript mark, not an approval mark, so that conversation can still reopen as the most recent. It refuses the caller's own session, which ends through its own end hook. The tool cannot check approval; the observer skill asks the user first.

**Reporting answers fast and says what happened.** A report names the calling session, never blocks, and says whether the observer received it. A report from an ended session is still recorded, its reply saying the session was previously ended and to run the start check; it never reopens, because only the check sees newer and open sessions. The tools track no restarts: a miss prompts the skill's one restart through start, and no port there gives the boot blocker.

## Good examples

**Halt, end, go.** A new session's start halts naming a session quiet for 40 minutes, maybe stuck, in the Build phase of a task. The user approves ending it; the agent ends it and calls start again, which now returns the link.

**Reopened as the most recent.** The user exits a session waiting at the plan gate and resumes it next morning; no other session has started since. Its first report is recorded and flagged previously ended, so the agent runs start, which finds this conversation's earlier session ended by its hook, nothing newer and nothing open, clears the mark and returns the link.

**Hard-stopped behind a newer session.** A conversation archived on Monday is un-archived on Thursday, after a Tuesday session built and ended. Its start finds Tuesday's session started later and answers with a hard stop: a newer session has worked the project since, nothing proceeds, continue in a new session. The agent tells the user and does nothing else.

## Bad examples

**A soft halt.** Start answers with a warning that another session may be active and no state; the agent reads it as advisory and starts planning in a project another session is building in.

**Judged by the fresh process.** A resumed conversation's start compares only its new observer session, started a minute ago, finds nothing newer and reopens it, though another session built and committed in between.

**A hard stop offered as a halt.** Start words a hard stop like a halt, so the agent asks the user to end the newer session; the user approves, and the stale conversation reopens over the newer one's work.

